# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Nothing here is a secret in local
# development: every value is either a fixed local address or a key the local
# Supabase stack prints on `supabase start`.
#
# Nothing needs to load this file by hand. Vite reads it because
# `apps/web/vite.config.ts` points `envDir` at the repo root, and the Supabase
# CLI reads it because it walks up from `supabase/` looking for `.env.local`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Supabase
# Run `supabase status` and copy the API URL and the publishable (anon) key.
#
# The server reads these same two variables, not a server-only pair. Both are
# public by design: the publishable key identifies the project and carries no
# privileges of its own, and every request is authorized by the signed-in
# user's JWT against RLS.
#
# There is deliberately no service-role key anywhere in this application. RLS
# is the authorization boundary, so the Worker must not hold a key that can
# bypass it. `supabase/tests/040_grants.test.sql` asserts that service_role
# cannot read a single row even if one were issued.
#
# Because Vite inlines PUBLIC_ variables, these must be present when
# `vite build` runs, not only when the Worker starts.
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
PUBLIC_APP_NAME="Résumé Studio"

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Google OAuth
# Google is the only sign-in method. supabase/config.toml reads these through
# env() substitution.
#
# Leaving them blank does more than disable sign-in: `supabase gen types` parses
# the whole config and refuses an enabled provider whose client id is empty, so
# `bun run db:types` fails until these are filled in.
#
# In the Google Cloud console, create an OAuth 2.0 Web application client and
# register this exact authorised redirect URI:
#   http://127.0.0.1:54321/auth/v1/callback
SUPABASE_AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
SUPABASE_AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- AI
# The chat route calls DeepSeek through the AI SDK. The key is a real secret
# and is read from `process.env` on the Worker, never through a PUBLIC_ name.
#
# It does not go in .env.local. Server secrets in `vite dev` come from the
# Cloudflare plugin, which reads `apps/web/.dev.vars` (gitignored); in
# production, `wrangler secret put DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`. Create the file with:
#   echo 'DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...' > apps/web/.dev.vars
#
# Model ids and base URL are plain config, set in `apps/web/wrangler.jsonc`
# under `vars`. They can also go in .dev.vars to override locally.
#   AI_MODEL_SMART=deepseek-v4-flash   # proposal runs
#   AI_MODEL_FAST=deepseek-v4-flash    # memory summaries
#   AI_BASE_URL=                       # only for a proxy or gateway
#
# Optional Jev decisions use @workspace/typesafe. Add TYPESAFE_API_KEY to
# apps/web/.dev.vars locally; use `wrangler secret put TYPESAFE_API_KEY`
# from apps/web in production. No key is required until the client is used.
# Do not put it in .env.local or a PUBLIC_ variable.
#   TYPESAFE_DEFAULT_MODEL=jev-latest  # plain config in wrangler.jsonc
#   TYPESAFE_BASE_URL=https://api.typesafe.ai  # optional API root override
